acility, and 2026 Revolving Credit Facility.\n22\nIn connection with the closing of the Kito Crosby Acquisition, the Company entered into a new senior secured term loan credit facility (\u201c2026 Term Loan B\u201d) and a new revolving credit facility with an expanded bank lender group (\u201c2026 Revolving Credit Facility\u201d) and issued 7.125% senior secured notes due 2033 (\u201cSenior Secured Notes\u201d). The 2026 Term Loan B, 2026 Revolving Credit Facility and Senior Secured Notes also replaced the Company\u2019s previous term loan credit facility (\u201c2024 Term Loan B\u201d) and revolving credit facility (\u201c2024 Revolving Credit Facility\u201d) and proceeds from the new credit facilities were used to fund the Kito Crosby Acquisition and fully repay the 2024 Term Loan B. There was no balance on the 2024 Revolving Credit Facility at the time of replacement by the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility.\n"}
{"page": 27, "text": "On September 21, 2026, the Company, Columbus McKinnon EMEA GmbH and certain subsidiary guarantors of the Company entered into its First Amendment (the \u201cFirst Amendment\u201d) to the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility Credit Agreement, dated as of February 3, 2026. The First Amendment reduced the applicable interest rate margin on the Term Loan B and 2026 Revolving Credit Facility by 50 basis points. Following the amendment, borrowings under the Term Loan B bear interest at Term Secured Overnight Financing Rate (\"SOFR\") plus 3.00%. All other material provisions, including maturity dates, remained unchanged. The Company evaluated the First Amendment and concluded the transaction should be accounted for primarily as a debt modification. The Company incurred $1,056,000 of costs associated with the debt modification including fees paid to third parties after the First Amendment went into effect.\nThe Company plans to pay down approximately $150,000,000 in borrowings in the next 12 month period. This amount has been recorded within the current portion of long-term debt on the Company's Condensed Consolidated Balance Sheet at September 30, 2026 with the remaining balance recorded as long-term debt.\nAs of September 30, 2026, the Company was in compliance with its covenants under the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility and Senior Secured Notes. The Company was in compliance with covenants under the AR Securitization Facility after receiving a waiver of the Delinquency and Default Ratios from the lender effective September 30, 2026. The AR Securitization Facility was further amended on October 29, 2026 to change the Delinquency and Default Ratios going forward. Accordingly, the amounts outstanding continue to be classified as long-term debt as of September 30, 2026.\nSenior Secured Notes\nOn January 30, 2026, the Company completed an offering of $900,000,000 in aggregate principal amount of 7.125% senior secured notes maturing February 1, 2033 in a private placement. The Senior Secured Notes bear interest at a rate of 7.125% per year payable semi-annually in cash in arrears on February 1 and August 1 of each year. The first such interest payment was made on August 1, 2026.\nGross deferred debt issuance costs related to the Senior Secured Notes were $13,973,000 as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026. The accumulated amortization balances were $1,331,000 and $333,000 as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026, respectively.\nNew 2026 Term Loan B\n"}
{"page": 28, "text": "On February 3, 2026, Columbus McKinnon Corporation (the \u201cBorrower\u201d) entered into a new credit agreement for a senior secured term loan credit facility or 2026 Term Loan B, in the initial amount of $1,650,000,000 maturing February 3, 2033, and a $500,000,000 2026 Revolving Credit Facility with a group of financial institutions or Revolving Credit Facility maturing February 3, 2031 (\u201c2026 Credit Agreement\u201d) with JPMorgan Chase Bank, N.A. as Administrative Agent and the lenders named therein. The 2026 Term Loan B replaced the Company\u2019s 2024 Term Loan B. Proceeds of the 2026 Term Loan B were used to complete the Kito Crosby Acquisition as well as fully repay the Company\u2019s 2024 Term Loan B.\nOn March 4, 2026, the Company repaid $191,080,000 of the 2026 Term Loan B using the proceeds of the divestiture of the Company\u2019s U.S. Power Chain Hoist and Chain Operations (See Note 2). The Company also received credit for a pro rata proportion of the Original Issuer Discount paid to the 2026 Term Loan B lenders in an amount of $1,900,000 resulting in an aggregate $193,010,000 reduction in the balance of the 2026 Term Loan B after completion of the Divestiture per the terms of the 2026 Credit Agreement.\nAs outlined in the 2026 Credit Agreement, prepayments can be made without penalty after August 3, 2026. On September 21, 2026, the Company entered into the First Amendment reducing the Company's applicable interest rate margin by 50 basis points. Following the effectiveness of the First Amendment, the Senior Credit Facilities bear interest at rates determined on the basis of either a term SOFR or a base rate plus an applicable margin. In the case of term SOFR loans under the 2026 Term Loan B Facility, such margin is 3.00% and in the case of dollar denominated term SOFR loans under the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility, such margin ranges from 1.75% to 2.75% based upon the Company\u2019s Consolidated Total Leverage Ratio (as defined in the 2026 Credit Agreement). 23\nAt September 30, 2026, the outstanding principal balance of the 2026 Term Loan B was $1,449,233,000 or $1,406,577,000 net of $42,656,000 of unamortized discount and debt issuance costs. Debt issuance costs are amortized over the contractual term to interest expense. The accumulated amortization balance was $4,462,000 as of September 30, 2026.\nThe Company made $3,632,000 in principal payments on the 2026 Term Loan B during the three months ended September 30, 2026. The Company is obligated to make $14,529,000 of principal payments on the 2026 Term Loan B facility over the next 12 months plus applicable Excess Cash Flow (\"ECF\") payments. The expected debt repayment on the Company's 2026 Term Loan B in the next 12 month period is included in the approximately $150,000,000 in debt repayment previously referenced herein.\n"}
{"page": 29, "text": "The gross balance of deferred financing costs on the 2026 Term Loan B facility was $47,118,000 as of September 30, 2026 and $46,839,000 as of March 31, 2026. The increase in the gross balance corresponds to additional fees incurred to lenders in for the First Amendment of $279,000. The accumulated amortization balance was $4,462,000 and $1,161,000 as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026, respectively.\n2026 Revolving Credit Facility\nOn February 3, 2026, the Company entered into a new $500,000,000 2026 Revolving Credit Facility with a group of financial institutions, maturing February 3, 2031 governed by the 2026 Credit Agreement. The 2026 Revolving Credit Facility replaced the Company\u2019s previous revolving credit facility, the 2024 Revolving Credit Facility.\nBorrowings under the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility bear interest at floating rates based upon indices determined by the currency of the borrowing or at an alternative base rate, in each case, plus an applicable margin. On September 21, 2026, the Company entered into the First Amendment. Following the First Amendment, the 2026 Senior Credit Facilities bear interest at rates determined on the basis of either a term SOFR or a base rate plus an applicable margin. For U.S. Dollar borrowings, these borrowings bear interest at applicable margin ranging from 1.75% to 2.75% based on Company's Consolidated Total Leverage Ratio as defined in the 2026 Credit Agreement.\nAs of September 30, 2026, there was $59,644,000 of borrowings outstanding under the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility on a US Dollar equivalent basis, including $38,461,000 borrowings outstanding in US Dollars and \u20ac18,700,000 borrowings outstanding in Euros. Availability under the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility was $421,080,000 net of $19,276,000 outstanding standby letters of credit issued against the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility and the borrowings outstanding.\nGross deferred debt issuance costs related to the 2026 Revolving Credit Facility were $7,038,000 as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026, which is included in Other assets on the Condensed Consolidated Balance Sheets. The accumulated amortization balances were $938,000 and $235,000 as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026, respectively.\nAR Securitization Facility\n"}
{"page": 30, "text": "Since June 20, 2023, the Company used an AR Securitization Facility secured by the Company\u2019s U.S. accounts receivable balances (the \u201cAR Securitization Facility\u201d) for general business purposes, including seasonal net working capital needs. On August 11, 2025, the AR Securitization Facility was amended to increase the maximum principal amount to $60,000,000 from the original borrowing base of $55,000,000, subject to a borrowing base calculation pursuant to the definitions and methodology outlined in the related credit agreement, eliminated the 10 basis point credit spread adjustment to the cost of borrowing, and extending its term through August 11, 2028. The AR Securitization Facility borrowings bear interest at a floating rate equal to a one-month SOFR rate plus a 110 basis points applicable margin.\nThe Company had $42,588,000 and $53,400,000 outstanding under its AR Securitization Facility as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026, respectively. The total U.S. accounts receivable balances which secure the AR Securitization Facility total $93,652,000 as of September 30, 2026.\nThe gross balance of deferred financing costs associated with the AR Securitization Facility was $273,000 with an accumulated amortization balance of $99,000 and $53,000 as of September 30, 2026 and March 31, 2026, respectively.\nThe Company has both the ability and intent to have the AR Securitization Facility remain outstanding for the next 12 months. As such, the Company has classified the full $42,588,000 outstanding borrowings under the AR Securitization Facility as long-term debt at September 30, 2026.\nAs of September 30, 2026, there have been no amortization events triggered in the AR Securitization Facility.\n24\nOther Debt\nOther debt of $5,183,000 includes a secured loan assumed in the Kito Crosby Acquisition that had previously been used to purchase a building in the United States. The loan matures on December 31, 2026 and has an interest rate of 2.7%. The full amount is included in the current portion of long-term debt.\nFinance Lease\nThe Company has one finance lease for a manufacturing facility in Hartland, WI under a 23-year lease agreement, which terminates in 2035. The outstanding balance on the finance lease obligations was $11,132,000 as of September 30, 2026 of which $851,000 has been recorded within the Current portion of long-term debt and finance lease obligations and the remaining balance recorded within the Term loan, Senior Secured Notes, AR securitization facility and finance lease obligations on the Condensed Consolidated Balance Sheets. Refer to Note 15 for further details.\nNon-U.S. Lines of Credit and Loans\n"}
{"page": 31, "text": "Unsecured and uncommitted lines of credit are available to meet short-term working capital needs for certain subsidiaries operating outside of the U.S. The lines of credit are available on an offering basis, meaning that transactions under the line of credit will be on such terms and conditions, including interest rate, maturity, representations, covenants and events of default, as mutually agreed between our subsidiaries and the local bank at the time of each specific transaction. As of September 30, 2026, unsecured credit lines totaled approximately $5,438,000, of which $566,000 was drawn. In addition, unsecured lines of $22,694,000 were available for bank guarantees issued in the normal course of business of which $18,757,000 was utilized as of September 30, 2026.\nRefer to the Company\u2019s consolidated financial statements included in the 2026 Form 10-K for further information on its debt arrangements.\n10. Net Periodic Benefit Cost\nThe following table sets forth the components of net periodic pension cost for the Company\u2019s defined benefit pension plans (in thousands):\nThree Months Ended Six Months Ended\nSeptember 30, 2026 September 30, 2025 September 30, 2026 September 30, 2025\nService costs $ 515 $ 113 $ 1,023 $ 223\nInterest cost 2,283 1,856 4,573 3,695\nExpected return on plan assets (1,482) (1,213) (2,959) (2,426)\nNet amortization (375) (249) (753) (492)\nNet periodic pension (benefit) cost $ 941 $ 507 $ 1,884 $ 1,000\nComponents of the net benefit costs other than the service cost component are recorded in Other (income) expense, net on the Condensed Consolidated Statements of Operations. Service costs are recorded as part of Income (loss) from operations. The three and six months ended September 30, 2026 includes the net periodic benefit cost of Kito Crosby, which was acquired in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2026.\nDuring fiscal year 2025, the Company terminated one of its U.S. pension plans. The remaining surplus of the terminated plan at September 30, 2026 of $2,843,000 is being used to fund certain obligations associated with the Company's U.S. defined contribution plans. Of the remaining balance, $2,210,000 is expected to be utilized in the next twelve months, and is therefore recorded in Prepaid expenses and other on the Condensed Consolidated Balance Sheets. The remaining balance is included in Other assets.\nThe Company currently plans to contribute approximately $7,341,000 to its pension plans in fiscal 2027.\nSubsequent to September 30, 2026, the Company settled one of its U.S. pension plans. As a result, the Company expects to reflect the impact of the settlement during fiscal 2027 which will be recorded in Other (income) expense, net on the Condensed Consolidated Statements of Operations. 25\n11. Earnings Per Share\nThe following table sets forth the computation of basic and diluted earnings per share (in thousands):\nThree Months Ended Six Months Ended\nSeptember 30, 2026 September 30, 2025 September 30, 2026 September 30, 2025\n"}
